{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.55,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R9"],
      "pins": ["J1.3", "M1A.IO0"],
      "nets": ["FLOAT_LOW", "FLOAT_HIGH"],
      "observed": "In the WATER LEVEL SENSOR/SWITCHES block, R6 (10 kΩ) is tied as a pull-up on the FLOAT_HIGH line and R9 is a 10 MΩ pulldown on the FLOAT_LOW line. The specification states the opposite biasing and a 10 kΩ value: 'R6 FLOAT_LOW +3V3 10k, R9 GND FLOAT_HIGH 10k'.",
      "expected": "Per the spec, FLOAT_LOW should carry the 10 kΩ pull-up (R6) and FLOAT_HIGH a 10 kΩ pulldown (R9). A 10 MΩ pulldown is three orders of magnitude off and, combined with the pull-up/pulldown swap, leaves the float-switch inputs weakly defined.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "with 10 kΩ/4.7 kΩ pull resistors visible in the .cir file: R6 FLOAT_LOW +3V3 10k, R9 GND FLOAT_HIGH 10k (§4.4)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Sheet 1, WATER LEVEL SENSOR/SWITCHES: 'R6 10k' from +3V3 to FLOAT_HIGH; 'R9 10M' from FLOAT_LOW to GND"}
      ],
      "impact": "The water-level float interlocks (low-level pump cutoff and high-level overflow stop, which also gate the pump/valve bases via R2/R11) become noise-sensitive and possibly inverted, undermining the ATO/pump safety logic.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    },
    {
      "category": "extra_component",
      "components": ["PR1", "H1", "C1"],
      "pins": ["PR1.VCC", "PR1.SDA", "PR1.SCL", "H1.1", "H1.3", "H1.4"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "I2C_SDA", "I2C_SCL"],
      "observed": "The TF-Luna LiDAR (PR1) is wired through header H1 to +5V, GND, I2C_SDA and I2C_SCL in the 'Water Height Sensor' block.",
      "expected": "The specification documents PR1 as excluded from the board and unwired: 'its pins are \"free\" and it is marked on_board: no / excluded from the board; it is not wired into any net', and water level is intended to be sensed by an ultrasonic sensor/floats, not the LiDAR.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "the r2 netlist shows PR1 with on_board: no, in_bom: yes, and all four pins (VCC, SDA, SCL, GND) typed free — i.e., it is not connected to any net in this design (§4.3)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Sheet 1, WATER LEVEL SENSOR/SWITCHES: PR1 'LiDAR' pins VCC/SDA/SCL/GND wired via H1 PB_L25mm to +5V, I2C_SDA, I2C_SCL"}
      ],
      "impact": "A 5 V LiDAR module is placed on the main I2C bus and powered although no firmware exists to drive it; this deviates from the intended (unpopulated) design and adds an unexpected bus load/device.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_component_type",
      "components": ["T5", "T3"],
      "pins": ["T5.1", "T5.2", "T5.3"],
      "nets": ["ATO_VALVE", "Dato"],
      "observed": "The ATO solenoid-valve low-side switch is implemented with T5 (AO3400A, a 30 V small-signal N-MOSFET), while T3 (AON6407) is instead used as the series MOSFET in the 24 V input reverse-polarity block.",
      "expected": "The specification assigns the ATO valve to the AON6407: 'T3 — AON6407 (8-DFN N-MOSFET) ATO solenoid-valve low-side switch ... drives ATO_VALVE net'. Switching a 24 V inductive solenoid with a 30 V-rated AO3400A leaves almost no margin for flyback transients.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "T3 — AON6407 (8-DFN N-MOSFET) | ATO solenoid-valve low-side switch | Netlist: drives ATO_VALVE net (§2)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Sheet 3, ATO Valve: 'ATO_VALVE' → R10 100Ω → T4 BT3904 → gate of T5 'AO3400A' switching the +24V solenoid; Sheet 1: T3 'AON6407' used for reverse polarity protection"}
      ],
      "impact": "The valve driver MOSFET is a lower-voltage part than intended; inductive spikes on the 24 V valve line can exceed its 30 V rating, risking failure of the ATO valve control.",
      "confidence": 0.4
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The build deviates from the specified design in at least the float-switch biasing (R9 is 10 MΩ instead of the specified 10 kΩ, with pull-up/pulldown roles swapped), and additionally shows the supposedly unwired TF-Luna LiDAR connected to the I2C bus and the ATO valve driven by an AO3400A instead of the specified AON6407; the float-interlock value error is the most concrete anomaly."
}

